>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

Approach: verify-then-dereference.  Helpers in
pintos/src/userprog/syscall.c:
  valid_byte (p)      p != NULL, p < PHYS_BASE and the page is mapped
                      (pagedir_get_page ()).
  valid_range (p, n)  n == 0 is accepted; otherwise rejects address
                      wrap-around, checks the first and last byte and
                      one byte of every page in between.
  valid_string (s)    scans to the NUL, re-checking at the start and on
                      each page boundary; gives up after one page.
The handler validates the stack word with the syscall number and the
1+N argument words (so sc-bad-sp and sc-bad-arg die before anything is
read), copies the arguments into a kernel array and dispatches.
Buffers (read/write) are checked with valid_range () and strings
(exec/create/open/remove) with valid_string () before any use and
before fs_lock is taken.  Any failure calls syscall_exit (-1).

Backstop (pintos/src/userprog/exception.c): kill () sets exit_code to
-1 before thread_exit (), so user-mode faults (bad-read, bad-jump, ...)
print "name: exit(-1)" via process_exit () and free their resources.
A kernel-mode page fault on a user address also terminates the process
instead of panicking the kernel.

Alternative rejected: get_user ()/put_user () with fault recovery is
faster but needs assembly helpers and careful release of resources
from the fault handler; the explicit checks are simpler to verify.

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full 4,096-byte page to be read
>> from or written to user memory.  What is the least number of
>> inspections of the page table (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that
>> might result?  What is the greatest number of inspections?  What
>> about for a system call that reads or writes two consecutive bytes?
>> Is your code capable of being modified to read or write only parts of
>> a page in user memory?

In valid_range (uaddr, size):
- For a 4,096-byte copy:
  - Least inspections: 2 calls to pagedir_get_page ().  If the buffer
    is page-aligned, valid_byte (start) and valid_byte (last) both
    check the same page.  The loop pg_round_down (start) + PGSIZE < last
    does not execute.  (Both ends checked: 2 calls).
  - Greatest inspections: 3 calls to pagedir_get_page ().  If the
    buffer is not page-aligned, start lies on page P0 and last lies on
    page P1.  valid_byte (start) checks P0 (1 call), valid_byte (last)
    checks P1 (1 call), and the loop checks the boundary page (1 call).
- For two consecutive bytes:
  - Exactly 2 inspections in both cases: valid_byte (start) and
    valid_byte (last).  The loop does not run.
- Partial page transfer:
  Our code validates the whole buffer upfront and aborts with exit(-1)
  if any byte is invalid, preserving atomic failure semantics.  It
  could be modified to read/write partial pages by chunking the
  transfer page-by-page until an unmapped address is encountered.

>> B5: How does your code ensure that wait() will fail for a given
>> process id if the process was not created by the calling process, or
>> if the calling process has already called wait() on that process?

The parent scans its own cur->children list looking for a child_status
whose tid matches child_tid.  If no matching record is found (e.g. invalid
tid, or a grandchild belonging to another thread's children list),
process_wait () immediately returns -1.  When a matching child_status is
found, the parent waits on cs->exit_sema, reads cs->exit_code, and
immediately removes cs from cur->children with list_remove (&cs->elem)
before releasing its reference.  Any subsequent call to wait () for the
same tid fails to find the entry and returns -1.

>> B6: Any access to user memory could fail if the user passed an
>> invalid pointer.  Why does your code not leak resources when this
>> happens?  Do you have to make any special effort to prevent resource
>> leaks in such cases?

Every user pointer, string, and buffer is validated before any kernel
resource (file descriptor, malloc heap memory, or global lock) is
allocated or acquired.  Specifically:
1. Syscall numbers and arguments are validated before reading.
2. User strings and buffers are validated before taking fs_lock.
3. If validation fails, syscall_exit (-1) invokes process_exit (),
   which unconditionally closes and frees all open files in fd_list,
   releases child status structures, signals exit status, and cleans
   up the page directory.
Because validation precedes resource acquisition and cleanup is
centralized in process_exit (), no resources can leak.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: How does your code ensure that exec() will not return until the
>> child process has successfully loaded its executable?  What
>> happens if the load fails?

process_execute () allocates a struct child_status with load_sema
initialized to 0 and load_ok initialized to false.  After calling
thread_create (), the parent blocks on sema_down (&cs->load_sema).
The child invokes load () inside start_process (); upon completion
(success or failure), it records the outcome in cs->load_ok, sets
cs->load_done = true, and calls sema_up (&cs->load_sema).  The parent
unblocks: if cs->load_ok is true, it returns the child's tid; if false,
it removes cs from cur->children, drops its reference, and returns
TID_ERROR (-1).  If the child is terminated or faults before completing
load, process_exit () detects !cs->load_done, sets cs->load_ok = false,
and ups load_sema so the parent never hangs.

>> B8: Consider parent process P and child process C.  Some possible
>> termination orders are:
>>   1. P terminates before C.
>>   2. C terminates before P calls wait() on C.
>>   3. C terminates after P calls wait() on C.
>> In each case, describe the events that occur, including the timing of
>> the release of the process's resources.

Each child_status has a ref_cnt initially set to 2 (one reference held
by P in cur->children, one held by C in cur->my_status), protected by
ref_lock.

1. P terminates before C:
   In process_exit (), P iterates through cur->children, removing each
   child_status and calling release_status (), decrementing ref_cnt to 1.
   P exits and its thread page is freed.  When C later terminates, C's
   process_exit () sets cs->exit_code, ups cs->exit_sema (unconsumed),
   and calls release_status (), decrementing ref_cnt to 0, which frees
   the struct child_status.  No zombie leak occurs.

2. C terminates before P calls wait() on C:
   In process_exit (), C sets cs->exit_code, calls sema_up (&cs->exit_sema),
   and calls release_status (), decrementing ref_cnt to 1.  C's thread
   page and user memory are completely freed, but child_status remains
   allocated in P's children list.  When P later calls process_wait (C),
   sema_down (&cs->exit_sema) succeeds immediately without blocking.
   P reads cs->exit_code, removes cs from cur->children, and calls
   release_status (), decrementing ref_cnt to 0 and freeing child_status.

3. C terminates after P calls wait() on C:
   P finds C in cur->children and blocks on sema_down (&cs->exit_sema).
   When C terminates, process_exit () records cs->exit_code, calls
   sema_up (&cs->exit_sema) waking P, and drops C's reference (ref_cnt
   becomes 1).  P unblocks, reads the exit code, removes cs from
   cur->children, and calls release_status (), decrementing ref_cnt to 0
   and freeing child_status.

>> B9: The file system is not thread-safe.  How do you protect it?

A single global fs_lock.  load () holds it for all of its file
accesses; fd_close () and fd_close_all () take it around file_close ().
User memory is validated before the lock is taken, and the lock is
never held while waiting for a child or creating a process, so it
cannot be part of a deadlock cycle.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B10: What is your rationale for choosing how to handle bad file
>> descriptors (e.g. negative values, descriptors not opened by this
>> process, stdin/stdout misuse)?

When given an invalid file descriptor (negative values, unknown/closed
descriptors, writing to stdin, or reading from stdout), our code
returns an error code (-1 for read/write/filesize/tell, false for close,
and ignores seek) rather than terminating the process.
Rationale:
1. Conforms to standard POSIX semantics (EBADF returns -1; not SIGSEGV).
2. A bad pointer violates memory protection and must kill the process,
   whereas an invalid descriptor is an ordinary runtime error that a
   well-behaved application can inspect, handle, and recover from.
3. Test suites (read-bad-fd, write-stdin, close-stdin, etc.) accept -1
   or false, allowing processes to continue executing normally.

>> B11: How does your implementation map a process id to a kernel
>> thread id?

Pintos adheres to a 1:1 threading model for user processes: each user
process corresponds directly to exactly one kernel thread.  Therefore,
the process id (pid_t) is identical to the kernel thread id (tid_t).
No translation table is necessary.

>> B12: How does your implementation prevent writes to running
>> executables?  Why is the file kept open rather than only holding
>> an inode or relying on an external lock?

In load () (pintos/src/userprog/process.c), after ELF segments and the
initial stack are successfully validated and loaded, the kernel calls
file_deny_write (file) and records the open handle in t->executable
without calling file_close (file) at the done: label.

Rationale and mechanisms:
1. Lifecycle of write-denial: Pintos ties inode write denial directly
   to the open struct file handle.  file_deny_write () sets
   file->deny_write = true and increments inode->deny_write_cnt.  When
   file_close () is invoked, it automatically calls file_allow_write (),
   which decrements inode->deny_write_cnt.  Closing the file at load-time
   would immediately undo write-denial.  Keeping the file open preserves
   protection for the process's entire execution lifetime.
2. Concurrent executions: Multiple concurrent processes running the same
   binary (e.g. rox-multichild) each open a distinct struct file for the
   same inode and call file_deny_write (), incrementing inode->deny_write_cnt.
   Writes remain disallowed until all instances have terminated and closed
   their respective handles.
3. Order-of-events synchronization: In process_exit (), cur->executable
   is closed under fs_lock before signaling cs->exit_sema to wake the
   parent.  This ensures that when a parent's wait () returns, writes
   to the child binary are guaranteed to be re-enabled on disk (verified
   by rox-child).
4. Failure safety: If load () fails at any point, the open file is closed
   immediately at done:, ensuring no handle leaks and that write-denial is
   not left active.  If a process terminates abnormally (e.g. killed by an
   exception), process_exit () is still executed by thread_exit (),
   reliably closing cur->executable under fs_lock.

